    "q-100279": {
      "dom_id": "step-q100279",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14198442/assets/a4f8b046dd85d258dd897e595b5dba1116e5df763e2e74a196c6b79e2a8a42a5.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x)^{3}(f(x)-4{x}^{2})^{5}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.",
      "worked_solution": "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=3.$ \n\nFrom the product rule, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x)^{3}(f(x)-4{x}^{2})^{5}={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(f(x)-4{x}^{2})^{5}.\n$$\n\nWe calculate the limits of each factor, separately.\n\nApplying the power rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x)^{3} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x))}^{3} \\\\  & =(3-1)^{3} \\\\  & ={2}^{3} \\\\  & =8,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(f(x)-4{x}^{2})^{4} & ={({lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(f(x)-4{x}^{2}))}^{5} \\\\  & =(3-4(1)^{2})^{5} \\\\  & =(-1)^{5} \\\\  & =-1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x)^{3}(f(x)-4{x}^{2})^{5} & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(3-x)^{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\,1}(f(x)-4{x}^{2})^{5} \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-8\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
