      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14198442/assets/b12ba04b803d58c6cacf4fc781db5c7032c3aedad80506c101bf084109ebd797.png)\n\nFind ${lim}_{x\\to -2}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{4(f(x))^{3}-3{x}^{2}}{2}}$ for the function $f(x)$ plotted above.",
      "worked_solution": "From the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)=-1.$\n\nApplying the root rule, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -2}\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{4(f(x))^{3}-3{x}^{2}}{2}} & =\\sqrt[3]{{lim}_{x\\to -2}(\\frac{4(f(x))^{3}-3{x}^{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{4\\cdot (-1)^{3}-3(-2)^{2}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{-4-12}{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{-8} \\\\  & =-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
